From 4f41bd708ff1eded753c890702b9c24b14a4e637 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 21:21:23 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] SignalDelegator: run the handler before rt_sigsuspend
 returns

The whole syscall body is a deferred-signal section
(DeferredSignalRefCountGuard at the top of HandleSyscallImpl), so the signal
that ends the host sigsuspend in GuestSigSuspend is only queued, to be
drained when the guard is destroyed. GuestSigSuspend restored the guest mask
before that, so at drain time HandleGuestSignal found the signal blocked by
the restored mask and parked it in PendingSignals: rt_sigsuspend returned
EINTR without running the handler, which Linux guarantees has run.

Trigger: the standard "block, check, sigsuspend" wait loop. A shell's `wait`
blocks SIGCHLD, reaps with WNOHANG, then sigsuspend()s with the old mask
until its SIGCHLD handler sets a flag; with the handler not run it sleeps
forever (under POWERarm, busybox ash's `(exit 4) & wait $!` hung this way).

GuestSigSuspend now drains the queue itself while the suspend mask is still
in effect, by leaving the deferred section for one fault-page store when it
is the outermost one, and only then restores the mask. Every queued frame is
delivered: each guest rt_sigreturn resumes the store, which faults again
while frames remain. errno is saved before the drain, whose handlers can
change it.

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, a static x86-64 test (SIGCHLD
handler, all signals blocked, fork a child that exits, sleep 100 ms,
sigsuspend(old)):
  before: sigsuspend ret=-1 errno=4 handler-ran-before-return=no
  after:  sigsuspend ret=-1 errno=4 handler-ran-before-return=yes
Linux (the same test on an aarch64 Raspberry Pi 5): yes.

Found in POWERarm, where busybox sh `wait` hung under the arm64 guest.
---
 .../LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp         | 20 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
index a59d376d4..3be680c4f 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
@@ -2439,6 +2439,24 @@ uint64_t SignalDelegator::GuestSigSuspend(FEX::HLE::ThreadStateObject* Thread, u
   // Spin this in a loop until we aren't sigsuspended
   // This can happen in the case that the guest has sent signal that we can't block
   uint64_t Result = sigsuspend(&HostSet);
+  const int SuspendErrno = errno;
+
+  // The signal that ended the suspend was only queued: the whole syscall body
+  // is a deferred-signal section (HandleSyscallImpl), and the queue normally
+  // drains when that section ends. By then the mask below has been restored,
+  // so a signal the caller blocks outside sigsuspend (the usual pattern, e.g.
+  // a shell's `wait`) went back to pending and its handler did not run
+  // before sigsuspend returned, as Linux guarantees. Drain it here instead,
+  // with the suspend mask still in effect. Only the outermost section may
+  // drain, which is the case unless FEX itself is nested around this call.
+  auto* CurrentFrame = Thread->Thread->CurrentFrame;
+  if (!Thread->SignalInfo.DeferredSignalFrames.empty() && CurrentFrame->State.DeferredSignalRefCount.Load() == 1) {
+    CurrentFrame->State.DeferredSignalRefCount.Decrement(1);
+    // Faults while any deferred frame is queued; each guest handler's
+    // rt_sigreturn resumes this store, which faults again for the next frame.
+    reinterpret_cast<FEXCore::Core::NonAtomicRefCounter<uint64_t>*>(CurrentFrame->InterruptFaultPagePtr)->Store(0);
+    CurrentFrame->State.DeferredSignalRefCount.Increment(1);
+  }
 
   // Restore Previous signal mask we are emulating
   // XXX: Might be unsafe if the signal handler adjusted the thread's signal mask
@@ -2448,7 +2466,7 @@ uint64_t SignalDelegator::GuestSigSuspend(FEX::HLE::ThreadStateObject* Thread, u
 
   CheckForPendingSignals(Thread);
 
-  return Result == -1 ? -errno : Result;
+  return Result == -1 ? -SuspendErrno : Result;
 }
 
 uint64_t SignalDelegator::GuestSigTimedWait(uint64_t* set, siginfo_t* info, const struct timespec* timeout, size_t sigsetsize) {
